{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"de06cd8b-0dc0-418e-8764-ae470f05f94c","status":"SUCCESS","response":"The cause of the error and the fix have been documented in [ANSWER.md](file:///tmp/h-1gxKVS/ANSWER.md).\n\n### Summary of Cause \u0026 Fix\n\n1. **Why the first publish worked but the second returned 500**:\n   - On the first publish, the DynamoDB table was empty. `similar` was an empty list (`[]`), and all other fields were strings/integers, which DynamoDB natively accepts.\n   - On the second publish, the table contained existing stories. [`similarity()`](file:///tmp/h-1gxKVS/src/app.py#L26-L32) computed a similarity score as a Python `float`. When [`publish()`](file:///tmp/h-1gxKVS/src/app.py#L48-L64) called `table.put_item(Item=item)`, boto3's DynamoDB serializer rejected the float with `TypeError: Float types are not supported. Use Decimal types instead.`, causing [`handler()`](file:///tmp/h-1gxKVS/src/app.py#L67-L77) to return 500.\n\n2. **Fix**:\n   - In [src/app.py](file:///tmp/h-1gxKVS/src/app.py#L26-L32), converted the similarity score return value from a `float` to `Decimal(str(round(...)))`.\n   - In [tests/test_publish.py](file:///tmp/h-1gxKVS/tests/test_publish.py#L28-L76), added tests verifying that publishing when stories already exist populates `similar` with valid `Decimal` scores and that sequential requests to `handler()` return HTTP 201 without failure.\n","duration_seconds":103.037879,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":100158,"output_tokens":11788,"thinking_tokens":7889,"cache_read_tokens":208053,"total_tokens":111946}}}
